module

public import Mathlib.FieldTheory.IntermediateField.Adjoin.Algebra
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.EtaleCohomology.GaloisCohomology.TranscendenceDegreeOneBrauer
import StacksCore.Tactic.EgaSgaAttribute

/-!
# The \(C_1\) property of an arbitrary transcendence-degree-one extension

Let `k` be an algebraically closed field and `K/k` a field extension of transcendence degree one,
*not* assumed finitely generated. Then `K` is a \(C_1\) field, and consequently its Brauer group
is trivial, as is the Brauer group of every finite extension of `K`.

The finitely generated case is Tsen's theorem, available here as
`isCField_one_of_essFiniteType_trdeg_eq_one`. The passage to the general case is made at the
level of the \(C_1\) property rather than of Brauer groups: a single homogeneous polynomial has
only finitely many coefficients, so it is defined over a finitely generated subextension, which
again has transcendence degree one once a transcendental element is thrown in. A nontrivial zero
over that subextension is a nontrivial zero over `K`.

## Main results

* `exists_intermediateField_essFiniteType_trdeg_eq_one`: finitely many elements of `K` lie in a
  finitely generated subextension of transcendence degree one.
* `isCField_one_of_trdeg_eq_one`: `K` is a \(C_1\) field.
* `brauerGroup_eq_one_of_trdeg_eq_one`: the Brauer group of `K` is trivial.
* `trdeg_eq_one_of_module_finite_of_trdeg_eq_one`: a finite extension of `K` again has
  transcendence degree one over `k`.
* `brauerGroup_eq_one_of_module_finite_of_trdeg_eq_one`: the Brauer group of every finite
  extension of `K` is trivial.

## Implementation notes

**Why the argument is run on \(C_1\) and not on Brauer groups.** The source's proof of Stacks
03RG writes `K'` as the filtered colimit of its finitely generated subextensions of
transcendence degree one and uses that the Brauer group commutes with that colimit. That
commutation is in neither this library nor Mathlib. The \(C_1\) property, by contrast, needs no
colimit statement at all: its defining condition quantifies over a single polynomial, and a
polynomial has a finite coefficient set. So the reduction is carried out one polynomial at a
time and `brauerGroup_eq_one_of_isCField` (Stacks 03RC) is applied at the end, to `K` itself.

**Throwing in a transcendental element.** The subextension generated by the coefficients alone
may be algebraic over `k`, and then it is `k` itself, for which the \(C_1\) property is not
among the results already in the library. Adjoining one transcendental element `t ∈ K` — which
exists because `Algebra.trdeg k K = 1 ≠ 0` — forces the subextension to have transcendence
degree exactly one: at least one because it contains `t`, at most one by the additivity of
transcendence degree in the tower `k ⊆ L ⊆ K` (`trdeg_add_eq`, Stacks 030H).

**Lifting the polynomial.** `MvPolynomial.mem_range_map_iff_coeffs_subset` produces the
polynomial over the subextension from the containment of its coefficient set, and homogeneity
transfers back along `MvPolynomial.coeff_map` because `algebraMap L K` is injective.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 03R9](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03R9): \(C_r\) fields.
* [Stacks Project, Tag 03RC](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03RC): a \(C_1\) field has
  trivial Brauer group.
* [Stacks Project, Tag 03RF](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03RF): Tsen's theorem in the
  finitely generated case.
* [Stacks Project, Tag 03RG](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03RG): the vanishing of
  `H^q(Spec K, 𝐆_m)`, whose Brauer-group input is what is proved here.
* [Stacks Project, Tag 030H](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/030H): additivity of the
  transcendence degree in a tower.
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

universe u v

/-- **Finitely many elements of a transcendence-degree-one extension lie in a finitely generated
subextension of transcendence degree one.** The subextension is generated by the given elements
together with one transcendental element, which is what pins its transcendence degree to one
rather than zero. -/
theorem exists_intermediateField_essFiniteType_trdeg_eq_one
    (k : Type u) [Field k] (K : Type v) [Field K] [Algebra k K]
    (h : Algebra.trdeg k K = 1) (s : Finset K) :
    ∃ L : IntermediateField k K, (s : Set K) ⊆ (L : Set K) ∧
      Algebra.EssFiniteType k L ∧ Algebra.trdeg k L = 1 := by
  classical
  have : Algebra.Transcendental k K := trdeg_ne_zero_iff.mp (by simp [h])
  obtain ⟨t, ht⟩ := Algebra.Transcendental.transcendental (R := k) (A := K)
  refine ⟨IntermediateField.adjoin k (↑(insert t s) : Set K), ?_, ?_, ?_⟩
  · refine subset_trans ?_ (IntermediateField.subset_adjoin k _)
    exact_mod_cast Finset.subset_insert t s
  · exact IntermediateField.essFiniteType_iff.mpr ⟨insert t s, rfl⟩
  · set L := IntermediateField.adjoin k (↑(insert t s) : Set K)
    have htL : t ∈ L := by
      refine IntermediateField.subset_adjoin k _ ?_
      exact_mod_cast Finset.mem_insert_self t s
    -- `L` is transcendental over `k`, since it contains the transcendental element `t`.
    have hLtr : Algebra.Transcendental k L := by
      refine ⟨⟨t, htL⟩, ?_⟩
      rw [← transcendental_algebraMap_iff (R := k) (S := L) (A := K)
        (FaithfulSMul.algebraMap_injective L K)]
      exact ht
    have hsum := trdeg_add_eq k L (A := K)
    rw [h] at hsum
    refine le_antisymm (hsum ▸ self_le_add_right _ _) ?_
    exact Cardinal.one_le_iff_ne_zero.mpr (trdeg_ne_zero_iff.mpr hLtr)

/-- **A transcendence-degree-one extension of an algebraically closed field is a \(C_1\) field**,
with no finite generation assumed. A homogeneous polynomial over `K` has finitely many
coefficients, so it is the image of a homogeneous polynomial over a finitely generated
subextension of transcendence degree one; that subextension is a \(C_1\) field by Tsen's theorem,
and a nontrivial zero there is a nontrivial zero over `K`. -/
@[sga "4½ Arcata 3.2.3"]
theorem isCField_one_of_trdeg_eq_one (k : Type u) [Field k] [IsAlgClosed k]
    (K : Type v) [Field K] [Algebra k K] (h : Algebra.trdeg k K = 1) : IsCField K 1 := by
  classical
  intro d n hdn f hf
  obtain ⟨L, hsub, hess, htr⟩ := exists_intermediateField_essFiniteType_trdeg_eq_one k K h f.coeffs
  have hinj : Function.Injective (algebraMap L K) := FaithfulSMul.algebraMap_injective L K
  obtain ⟨f₀, hf₀⟩ : f ∈ Set.range (MvPolynomial.map (algebraMap L K : L →+* K)) := by
    refine MvPolynomial.mem_range_map_iff_coeffs_subset.mpr fun x hx ↦ ?_
    exact ⟨⟨x, hsub hx⟩, rfl⟩
  have hhom : f₀.IsHomogeneous d := by
    intro ν hν
    refine hf (d := ν) ?_
    rw [← hf₀, MvPolynomial.coeff_map]
    exact fun hc ↦ hν (hinj (by rw [hc, map_zero]))
  obtain ⟨α, hα, hαz⟩ :=
    isCField_one_of_essFiniteType_trdeg_eq_one k L htr d n hdn f₀ hhom
  refine ⟨fun i ↦ algebraMap L K (α i), ?_, ?_⟩
  · refine fun hzero ↦ hα (funext fun i ↦ hinj ?_)
    simpa using congrFun hzero i
  · have key : algebraMap L K (MvPolynomial.eval α f₀) =
        MvPolynomial.eval (fun i ↦ algebraMap L K (α i))
          (MvPolynomial.map (algebraMap L K : L →+* K) f₀) := by
      rw [MvPolynomial.eval_map]
      simpa [Function.comp_def, MvPolynomial.eval₂_id] using
        MvPolynomial.eval₂_comp_left (algebraMap L K) (RingHom.id L) α f₀
    rw [← hf₀, ← key, hαz, map_zero]

/-- **The Brauer group of a transcendence-degree-one extension of an algebraically closed field
is trivial**, with no finite generation assumed. This is Stacks 03RC applied to
`isCField_one_of_trdeg_eq_one`. -/
@[sga "4½ Arcata 3.2.2" "special case"]
theorem brauerGroup_eq_one_of_trdeg_eq_one (k : Type u) [Field k] [IsAlgClosed k]
    (K : Type v) [Field K] [Algebra k K] (h : Algebra.trdeg k K = 1) (y : BrauerGroup K) :
    y = 1 :=
  brauerGroup_eq_one_of_isCField K (isCField_one_of_trdeg_eq_one k K h) y

/-- **A finite extension of a transcendence-degree-one extension again has transcendence degree
one**, by the additivity of the transcendence degree in a tower and the vanishing of the
transcendence degree of an algebraic extension. -/
theorem trdeg_eq_one_of_module_finite_of_trdeg_eq_one (k : Type u) [Field k]
    (K : Type v) [Field K] [Algebra k K] (h : Algebra.trdeg k K = 1)
    (K' : Type v) [Field K'] [Algebra K K'] [Module.Finite K K']
    [Algebra k K'] [IsScalarTower k K K'] :
    Algebra.trdeg k K' = 1 := by
  rw [← trdeg_add_eq k K (A := K'), h, trdeg_eq_zero, add_zero]

/-- **The Brauer group of every finite extension of a transcendence-degree-one extension of an
algebraically closed field is trivial.**

This is the shape in which the vanishing criterion of Stacks 03R8 consumes Tsen's theorem in the
proof of Stacks 03RG: that criterion asks for the Brauer group of every finite extension of the
field, not just of the field itself. Compared with
`brauerGroup_eq_one_of_module_finite_of_essFiniteType_trdeg_eq_one`, no finite generation is
assumed of `K/k` here, which is what the hypothesis of Stacks 03RG actually provides. -/
@[sga "4½ Arcata 3.2.2" "special case"]
theorem brauerGroup_eq_one_of_module_finite_of_trdeg_eq_one (k : Type u) [Field k] [IsAlgClosed k]
    (K : Type v) [Field K] [Algebra k K] (h : Algebra.trdeg k K = 1)
    (K' : Type v) [Field K'] [Algebra K K'] [Module.Finite K K']
    [Algebra k K'] [IsScalarTower k K K'] (y : BrauerGroup K') : y = 1 :=
  brauerGroup_eq_one_of_trdeg_eq_one k K'
    (trdeg_eq_one_of_module_finite_of_trdeg_eq_one k K h K') y

end
